{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794532182002,"sessionID":"ses_e2d8e4f65ffeV4WrEmvaY5YjgO","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2740600001UwZDahjeFKBIMX","sessionID":"ses_e2d8e4f65ffeV4WrEmvaY5YjgO","messageID":"msg_1d273ffab001V10mxur07iWQj6","type":"tool","id":"call_05098923b2c943ada84082be","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/synthesis-review.md","offset":100,"limit":90},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/synthesis-review.md, lines 100-189\n100: | C53 | The run's config carried two disagreeing stop-policy fields | UNVERIFIED | I did not open `lineages/codex-luna/deep-research-config.json:3-9` and `:96` |\n101: \n102: ### 2.7 Premises under the recommendations\n103: \n104: | # | Premise | Status | Evidence |\n105: |---|---|---|---|\n106: | C54 | R1: the command tree plus each hub's `mode-registry.json` describes every command | WRONG | Only three registries carry a command (`sk-doc`, `sk-design`, `system-deep-loop`), covering 19. `system-spec-kit`, `sk-prompt` and `system-skilled-doctor` have no `mode-registry.json`. A deriver that fails \"when a live command is in neither\" fails on 26 commands on its first run |\n107: | C55 | R2: every command has `userIntent.ownedSignals` to generate phrases from | WRONG | Only the 19 metadata entries have the field. The matcher needs the whole phrase, contiguous, on word boundaries (`explicit.ts:242-263`). `/deep:research` owns \"investigate a topic iteratively\", \"run a research convergence loop\" and \"gather deep technical findings\" (`.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/command-metadata.json:9-13`). None occurs in \"research how our retrieval scoring works in depth\". `/speckit:plan` has no metadata at all |\n108: | C56 | R2: enabling each command's TypeScript row is the route to plain-chat reach | Contradicts a recorded design rule the synthesis cited without reading it that way | `sk-doc/mode-registry.json:12`: modes are \"resolved by hub membership\", a command-bridge mode is \"never advisor-scored\", and there are \"No lexical/alias-fold modes (avoids the advisor-projection extension + CI drift-guard)\" |\n109: | C57 | The compiled route is not mentioned anywhere in the synthesis | Missing fact | `advisor_recommend` with `includeCompiledRoute: true` returns the resolved mode: `create a readme for the utils folder` gives `sk-doc` with `workflowMode: sk-create-readme`. `make a chart of monthly revenue by region` gives `sk-design-chart`. `run deep research on caching strategies` gives `research`. A direct call to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs` took 39 to 56 ms in seven runs. The hook turns it off (`.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:221`) |\n110: \n111: ### 2.8 Can a model start a slash command itself\n112: \n113: | Runtime | Finding | Basis |\n114: |---|---|---|\n115: | Claude Code | Yes for 33 of 45 by name. No for 12 create commands. `/create:skill` is reachable as `create` | Observed in this session, live test above. Judgment on cause: `create/skill.md` collides with the `SKILL.md` convention on a case-insensitive disk, so the folder reads as one skill. Renaming the file in a scratch checkout and relisting would confirm it |\n116: | OpenCode | No documented model path. A parent process can start one with `opencode run --command` | `.opencode/SYNC.md:66`. Search summary of `opencode.ai/docs/commands`: commands are user-triggered templates, while skills are loaded by the model through a skill tool |\n117: | Codex | No. The mirror is a pointer stub that tells the model to read the command file | `.codex/SYNC.md:35-44`. There is no `.codex/skills` |\n118: | Pi | No model path found. The mirror is a pointer stub | `.pi/SYNC.md:27`, `:80-83`. Public Pi docs not fetched, so UNVERIFIED beyond the repository |\n119: | Cursor | No model path found, and the brief is dormant | `.cursor/SYNC.md:32`, `:84`. Public Cursor docs not fetched |\n120: | Devin | No command surface at all. Skills are exposed | `.devin/SYNC.md:20` |\n121: | DSH | No. \"dsh has no agent-file loader and no command loader\" | `.dsh/SYNC.md:49` |\n122: \n123: The one instruction that works on all seven is \"read the command document and follow it\". It is also what the typed path already does on Codex, Pi and DSH.\n124: \n125: ---\n126: \n127: ## 3. THE FULL RECOMMENDATION SET\n128: \n129: Order of entries: the ten merged recommendations, then recommendations a lineage made that the merge dropped or softened (L1 to L12). Gaps are in section 4. Priority words: must, should, could.\n130: \n131: ### R1. One generated inventory\n132: \n133: - **Merged text.** Derive the inventory from the command tree plus each hub's `mode-registry.json`. Fail when a live command is in neither or a bridge names a missing spelling. Keep route-level targets beneath the command.\n134: - **Verdict: AMEND. Priority: must.**\n135: - **Change.** The command tree owns identity, meaning name and existence. Attributes live with the owner: `command-metadata.json` in the owning skill, and `routes` for doctor. Add `command-metadata.json` to `system-spec-kit` (seven commands) and `sk-prompt` (one). In `bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs`, read the tracked files under `.skilled/commands/*/` and fail when a tracked command has no metadata entry or doctor route, and when an entry names a command with no file. Use the registry `command` field only as a cross-check. Keep one record per doctor route.\n136: - **Evidence.** The tree is the only complete source and every mirror generator already reads it (`.codex/SYNC.md:29`, `.pi/SYNC.md:27`, `.dsh/SYNC.md:32`, `.claude/SYNC.md:26`, `.cursor/SYNC.md:32`). The deriver already reads any skill's metadata file (`derive-command-bridges.cjs:78-114`). The allow-list itself says the rows exist because \"system-spec-kit owns the live planning command but has no command-metadata.json yet\" (`bridges/allow-list.json:7`). C54 shows why registries cannot be the second source.\n137: - **This settles the identity question.** The codex lineage held ownership open. Judgment: the tree owns identity because it is the only source where a missing row means the command does not exist. Would change it: a command that must exist without a file.\n138: - **Depends on.** Nothing. First build phase after the baseline.\n139: - **Proof.** `node bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs --check` prints `\"status\": \"fresh\"`. Add a fixture command file with no metadata and the same check exits 1 naming it. Remove a command file and the check names the orphan entry.\n140: \n141: ### R2. Plain-chat reach\n142: \n143: - **Merged text.** Keep the threshold. Generate plain-language phrases from `userIntent.ownedSignals`, enable every TypeScript row, keep the 0.08 adjustment as a tie-break and add a same-owner precedence rule.\n144: - **Verdict: REPLACE the mechanism, keep the threshold. Priority: must.**\n145: - **Change, part A: attach, for the 19 hub commands.** Keep the owner skill as the scored candidate. When a compiled hub resolves one mode (`action: route`, `selectionKind: single`) and that mode's registry row has a `command`, attach the command to the skill's recommendation. Files: `advisor/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` (`enrichCompiledRoutes` and `publicRecommendation`), a small registry lookup the deriver can generate, and `hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:221` so the hook asks for the route of the top passing hub. Leave the 17 hub rows disabled as scoring candidates.\n146: - **Change, part B: phrases, for the 8 commands with no hub mode.** Author intent phrases in the new `system-spec-kit` and `sk-prompt` metadata files, written as words users type and checked against chat-style corpus rows. Project them onto one row per command. This replaces the umbrella `command-spec-kit` row and the two retired memory ids.\n147: - **Evidence for A.** C57: the mode resolves today in about 50 ms. The owner skill already passes for plain prompts: `create a readme for the utils folder` gives `sk-doc` 0.94, `write a changelog entry for this release` gives `sk-doc` 0.89, `make a chart of monthly revenue by region` gives `sk-design` 0.82, `run deep research on caching strategies` gives `system-deep-loop` 0.88. Commands as candidates misbehave today: a typed `/speckit:plan` is ambiguous with its owner, `save context` spends two of three slots on two ids for one command, and `create:agent` at 0.82 sorts above `sk-code` at 0.95. C56: the sk-doc registry rules out advisor-scored modes.\n148: - **Evidence for B.** `system-spec-kit` is not a compiled hub (its `compiledRoute` was null in my run) and has no registry, so its seven commands need their own rows. C55: generated `ownedSignals` cannot be the source.\n149: - **Judgment.** Attach beats compete for hub commands. Would change it: a baseline showing owner-skill recall for chat prompts below command-row recall after phrases are added.\n150: - **Depends on.** R1 and the R8 baseline.\n151: - **Proof.** Against the captured baseline: `plan a new feature for the login page` returns `/speckit:plan`. `create a readme for the utils folder` returns `sk-doc` with command `/create:readme`. `full_corpus_top1` stays at or above 158/195, `holdout_top1` at or above 50/66 and `gold_none_false_fire` at or below 5. A typed `/speckit:plan` is no longer `ambiguous: true`.\n152: \n153: ### R3. Output contract and brief\n154: \n155: - **Merged text.** Add `kind`, `command`, `policy` and `ownerSkillId`. Render `use <skill> → <command> (<policy>)` in the head for every family. Do not raise the cap.\n156: - **Verdict: AMEND. Priority: must.**\n157: - **Change.** In `advisor/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:294-309` add `command` (the runnable string with its route target), `policy` (`run`, `start` or `ask`), `ownerSkillId` (only on a row that is itself a command bridge) and `kind` (already present internally, copy it through at `advisor-recommend.ts:306-350`). Collapse rows that share one command into one entry before the top-K cut. In `advisor/lib/render.ts`, replace the doctor-only map at `:109-150` with a generated map for every command and put the command and policy in the required head, not the `Also:` line. Extend the deriver's `renderDoctorCommandStrings` (`derive-command-bridges.cjs:591-604`).\n158: - **Evidence.** C18, C22, C23. The `Also:` line is dropped when over the cap (`render.ts:524-527`), so anything the model must act on belongs in the head, as the codex lineage said. Duplicate ids crowd the list today (`save context` run).\n159: - **Depends on.** R1, R4.\n160: - **Proof.** A schema test accepts the four fields and rejects an unknown fifth. Golden briefs, one per family, each at or under 320 characters before the directive block. `save context` returns one entry for `/speckit:save`. A daemon on the old build still answers: the CLI already retries once without a rejected option (`skill-advisor-hook.md:38`), and a test should pin that for the new fields.\n161: \n162: ### R4. Risk class and policy\n163: \n164: - **Merged text.** Carry a risk class and write target in each command's metadata and derive `policy` from it. Strictness left open.\n165: - **Verdict: AMEND. Priority: must.**\n166: - **Change.** Reuse the doctor enum `mutating: read-only | add-only | mutates` and `gate3_location` (`routes:40-41`) in every `command-metadata.json` entry. Add one optional list, `ask_first`, with four allowed reasons: `settings`, `outside-repo`, `spend`, `relaunch`. The deriver computes `policy` and fails on an unknown reason or a missing class. Give `/doctor:env` per-action classes the way `/doctor:update` already has them (`routes:740-758`).\n167: - **The rule, in two sentences.** Run a route outright when it declares `read-only`, or `add-only` with every write inside the bound spec folder. Ask one question first when the route changes settings or installs, touches anything outside tracked files, spawns executors or iterations or relaunches the session, and start every remaining route without `:auto` so its own setup, Gate 3 and checkpoints do the asking.\n168: - **Mechanical form.** `ask_first` non-empty gives `ask`. A doctor route classed `mutates` gives `ask`. `read-only`, or `add-only` into the spec folder, gives `run`. Everything else gives `start`.\n169: - **Evidence.** The repository already ships this split for a model-chosen command: `/doctor:helper` \"runs a read-only pick under that command's own router and prints a writing pick as one line for the operator\" (`routes:458`), and a default target is \"never on a mutating route\" (`routes:46-50`). A tracked-file edit is the top tier of the reversibility ladder, \"Proceed\" (`.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md:63`), and \"an install still waits for a yes\" (`:64`). Gate 3 fires on every write whatever the command does (`AGENTS.md:73`), so a second question before it breaks the single-prompt rule in `AGENTS.md` section 2. An omitted mode lands on confirm or a mode question (C27).\n170: - **How this settles the lineage split.** The strict line asks 34 times, mostly for writes the user's own sentence requested. The ladder line runs `/speckit:implement` with no question. The merged \"start\" is right for the middle band but wrong to lean on the command's setup alone (C30). The rule above keeps \"start\" only where an independent gate exists, and asks where none does.\n171: - **Judgment.** The middle band is a preference, see section 7. Would change it: an R8 behaviour run where a started create or speckit command skips its setup, or an unsafe-start rate above zero.\n172: - **Depends on.** R1.\n173: - **Proof.** The deriver emits a policy for all 44 tracked commands and every doctor route. A table-driven test asserts the four mechanical rows. Changing a doctor route from `read-only` to `mutates` flips its policy to `ask` in the generated file.\n174: \n175: ### R5. Invocation per runtime\n176: \n177: - **Merged text.** One command identity with a per-runtime adapter. Use a model-invocable command where the host has one. Elsewhere load the command document and follow it, or ask the user to type it. Derive the runtime list from the hook registrations.\n178: - **Verdict: AMEND. Priority: must.**\n179: - **Change.** Make \"read the command document in full and follow it\" the single instruction on every runtime. Treat Claude Code's Skill tool as an optional shortcut for the 33 names it lists. Never tell the model to type a slash command. Ask the user to type one only when the model cannot read the file. Document the per-runtime table from section 2.8 in `skill-advisor-hook.md` and add the DSH row.\n180: - **Evidence.** Section 2.8. The typed path on Codex, Pi and DSH is already this instruction (C35). The Skill tool misses 12 create commands (C33).\n181: - **Depends on.** R3.\n182: - **Proof.** A behaviour run per runtime: a chat prompt that should reach `/create:readme` ends with `create-readme` workflow YAML read before the first write. On Claude Code the same holds for a create command the Skill tool does not list.\n183: \n184: ### R6. The Gate 2 sentence\n185: \n186: - **Merged text.** Three layers: the brief carries command and policy, one sentence in Gate 2 binds the behaviour, the command file is the body.\n187: - **Verdict: AMEND. Priority: must. Not sufficient alone.**\n188: - **Change.** Add one bullet to Gate 2 in `AGENTS.md` after line 67. Proposed text, about 430 bytes:\n189: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 190]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/synthesis-review.md, lines 100-189\n100: | C53 | The run's config carried two disagreeing stop-policy fields | UNVERIFIED | I did not open `lineages/codex-luna/deep-research-config.json:3-9` and `:96` |\n101: \n102: ### 2.7 Premises under the recommendations\n103: \n104: | # | Premise | Status | Evidence |\n105: |---|---|---|---|\n106: | C54 | R1: the command tree plus each hub's `mode-registry.json` describes every command | WRONG | Only three registries carry a command (`sk-doc`, `sk-design`, `system-deep-loop`), covering 19. `system-spec-kit`, `sk-prompt` and `system-skilled-doctor` have no `mode-registry.json`. A deriver that fails \"when a live command is in neither\" fails on 26 commands on its first run |\n107: | C55 | R2: every command has `userIntent.ownedSignals` to generate phrases from | WRONG | Only the 19 metadata entries have the field. The matcher needs the whole phrase, contiguous, on word boundaries (`explicit.ts:242-263`). `/deep:research` owns \"investigate a topic iteratively\", \"run a research convergence loop\" and \"gather deep technical findings\" (`.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/command-metadata.json:9-13`). None occurs in \"research how our retrieval scoring works in depth\". `/speckit:plan` has no metadata at all |\n108: | C56 | R2: enabling each command's TypeScript row is the route to plain-chat reach | Contradicts a recorded design rule the synthesis cited without reading it that way | `sk-doc/mode-registry.json:12`: modes are \"resolved by hub membership\", a command-bridge mode is \"never advisor-scored\", and there are \"No lexical/alias-fold modes (avoids the advisor-projection extension + CI drift-guard)\" |\n109: | C57 | The compiled route is not mentioned anywhere in the synthesis | Missing fact | `advisor_recommend` with `includeCompiledRoute: true` returns the resolved mode: `create a readme for the utils folder` gives `sk-doc` with `workflowMode: sk-create-readme`. `make a chart of monthly revenue by region` gives `sk-design-chart`. `run deep research on caching strategies` gives `research`. A direct call to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs` took 39 to 56 ms in seven runs. The hook turns it off (`.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:221`) |\n110: \n111: ### 2.8 Can a model start a slash command itself\n112: \n113: | Runtime | Finding | Basis |\n114: |---|---|---|\n115: | Claude Code | Yes for 33 of 45 by name. No for 12 create commands. `/create:skill` is reachable as `create` | Observed in this session, live test above. Judgment on cause: `create/skill.md` collides with the `SKILL.md` convention on a case-insensitive disk, so the folder reads as one skill. Renaming the file in a scratch checkout and relisting would confirm it |\n116: | OpenCode | No documented model path. A parent process can start one with `opencode run --command` | `.opencode/SYNC.md:66`. Search summary of `opencode.ai/docs/commands`: commands are user-triggered templates, while skills are loaded by the model through a skill tool |\n117: | Codex | No. The mirror is a pointer stub that tells the model to read the command file | `.codex/SYNC.md:35-44`. There is no `.codex/skills` |\n118: | Pi | No model path found. The mirror is a pointer stub | `.pi/SYNC.md:27`, `:80-83`. Public Pi docs not fetched, so UNVERIFIED beyond the repository |\n119: | Cursor | No model path found, and the brief is dormant | `.cursor/SYNC.md:32`, `:84`. Public Cursor docs not fetched |\n120: | Devin | No command surface at all. Skills are exposed | `.devin/SYNC.md:20` |\n121: | DSH | No. \"dsh has no agent-file loader and no command loader\" | `.dsh/SYNC.md:49` |\n122: \n123: The one instruction that works on all seven is \"read the command document and follow it\". It is also what the typed path already does on Codex, Pi and DSH.\n124: \n125: ---\n126: \n127: ## 3. THE FULL RECOMMENDATION SET\n128: \n129: Order of entries: the ten merged recommendations, then recommendations a lineage made that the merge dropped or softened (L1 to L12). Gaps are in section 4. Priority words: must, should, could.\n130: \n131: ### R1. One generated inventory\n132: \n133: - **Merged text.** Derive the inventory from the command tree plus each hub's `mode-registry.json`. Fail when a live command is in neither or a bridge names a missing spelling. Keep route-level targets beneath the command.\n134: - **Verdict: AMEND. Priority: must.**\n135: - **Change.** The command tree owns identity, meaning name and existence. Attributes live with the owner: `command-metadata.json` in the owning skill, and `routes` for doctor. Add `command-metadata.json` to `system-spec-kit` (seven commands) and `sk-prompt` (one). In `bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs`, read the tracked files under `.skilled/commands/*/` and fail when a tracked command has no metadata entry or doctor route, and when an entry names a command with no file. Use the registry `command` field only as a cross-check. Keep one record per doctor route.\n136: - **Evidence.** The tree is the only complete source and every mirror generator already reads it (`.codex/SYNC.md:29`, `.pi/SYNC.md:27`, `.dsh/SYNC.md:32`, `.claude/SYNC.md:26`, `.cursor/SYNC.md:32`). The deriver already reads any skill's metadata file (`derive-command-bridges.cjs:78-114`). The allow-list itself says the rows exist because \"system-spec-kit owns the live planning command but has no command-metadata.json yet\" (`bridges/allow-list.json:7`). C54 shows why registries cannot be the second source.\n137: - **This settles the identity question.** The codex lineage held ownership open. Judgment: the tree owns identity because it is the only source where a missing row means the command does not exist. Would change it: a command that must exist without a file.\n138: - **Depends on.** Nothing. First build phase after the baseline.\n139: - **Proof.** `node bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs --check` prints `\"status\": \"fresh\"`. Add a fixture command file with no metadata and the same check exits 1 naming it. Remove a command file and the check names the orphan entry.\n140: \n141: ### R2. Plain-chat reach\n142: \n143: - **Merged text.** Keep the threshold. Generate plain-language phrases from `userIntent.ownedSignals`, enable every TypeScript row, keep the 0.08 adjustment as a tie-break and add a same-owner precedence rule.\n144: - **Verdict: REPLACE the mechanism, keep the threshold. Priority: must.**\n145: - **Change, part A: attach, for the 19 hub commands.** Keep the owner skill as the scored candidate. When a compiled hub resolves one mode (`action: route`, `selectionKind: single`) and that mode's registry row has a `command`, attach the command to the skill's recommendation. Files: `advisor/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` (`enrichCompiledRoutes` and `publicRecommendation`), a small registry lookup the deriver can generate, and `hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:221` so the hook asks for the route of the top passing hub. Leave the 17 hub rows disabled as scoring candidates.\n146: - **Change, part B: phrases, for the 8 commands with no hub mode.** Author intent phrases in the new `system-spec-kit` and `sk-prompt` metadata files, written as words users type and checked against chat-style corpus rows. Project them onto one row per command. This replaces the umbrella `command-spec-kit` row and the two retired memory ids.\n147: - **Evidence for A.** C57: the mode resolves today in about 50 ms. The owner skill already passes for plain prompts: `create a readme for the utils folder` gives `sk-doc` 0.94, `write a changelog entry for this release` gives `sk-doc` 0.89, `make a chart of monthly revenue by region` gives `sk-design` 0.82, `run deep research on caching strategies` gives `system-deep-loop` 0.88. Commands as candidates misbehave today: a typed `/speckit:plan` is ambiguous with its owner, `save context` spends two of three slots on two ids for one command, and `create:agent` at 0.82 sorts above `sk-code` at 0.95. C56: the sk-doc registry rules out advisor-scored modes.\n148: - **Evidence for B.** `system-spec-kit` is not a compiled hub (its `compiledRoute` was null in my run) and has no registry, so its seven commands need their own rows. C55: generated `ownedSignals` cannot be the source.\n149: - **Judgment.** Attach beats compete for hub commands. Would change it: a baseline showing owner-skill recall for chat prompts below command-row recall after phrases are added.\n150: - **Depends on.** R1 and the R8 baseline.\n151: - **Proof.** Against the captured baseline: `plan a new feature for the login page` returns `/speckit:plan`. `create a readme for the utils folder` returns `sk-doc` with command `/create:readme`. `full_corpus_top1` stays at or above 158/195, `holdout_top1` at or above 50/66 and `gold_none_false_fire` at or below 5. A typed `/speckit:plan` is no longer `ambiguous: true`.\n152: \n153: ### R3. Output contract and brief\n154: \n155: - **Merged text.** Add `kind`, `command`, `policy` and `ownerSkillId`. Render `use <skill> → <command> (<policy>)` in the head for every family. Do not raise the cap.\n156: - **Verdict: AMEND. Priority: must.**\n157: - **Change.** In `advisor/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts:294-309` add `command` (the runnable string with its route target), `policy` (`run`, `start` or `ask`), `ownerSkillId` (only on a row that is itself a command bridge) and `kind` (already present internally, copy it through at `advisor-recommend.ts:306-350`). Collapse rows that share one command into one entry before the top-K cut. In `advisor/lib/render.ts`, replace the doctor-only map at `:109-150` with a generated map for every command and put the command and policy in the required head, not the `Also:` line. Extend the deriver's `renderDoctorCommandStrings` (`derive-command-bridges.cjs:591-604`).\n158: - **Evidence.** C18, C22, C23. The `Also:` line is dropped when over the cap (`render.ts:524-527`), so anything the model must act on belongs in the head, as the codex lineage said. Duplicate ids crowd the list today (`save context` run).\n159: - **Depends on.** R1, R4.\n160: - **Proof.** A schema test accepts the four fields and rejects an unknown fifth. Golden briefs, one per family, each at or under 320 characters before the directive block. `save context` returns one entry for `/speckit:save`. A daemon on the old build still answers: the CLI already retries once without a rejected option (`skill-advisor-hook.md:38`), and a test should pin that for the new fields.\n161: \n162: ### R4. Risk class and policy\n163: \n164: - **Merged text.** Carry a risk class and write target in each command's metadata and derive `policy` from it. Strictness left open.\n165: - **Verdict: AMEND. Priority: must.**\n166: - **Change.** Reuse the doctor enum `mutating: read-only | add-only | mutates` and `gate3_location` (`routes:40-41`) in every `command-metadata.json` entry. Add one optional list, `ask_first`, with four allowed reasons: `settings`, `outside-repo`, `spend`, `relaunch`. The deriver computes `policy` and fails on an unknown reason or a missing class. Give `/doctor:env` per-action classes the way `/doctor:update` already has them (`routes:740-758`).\n167: - **The rule, in two sentences.** Run a route outright when it declares `read-only`, or `add-only` with every write inside the bound spec folder. Ask one question first when the route changes settings or installs, touches anything outside tracked files, spawns executors or iterations or relaunches the session, and start every remaining route without `:auto` so its own setup, Gate 3 and checkpoints do the asking.\n168: - **Mechanical form.** `ask_first` non-empty gives `ask`. A doctor route classed `mutates` gives `ask`. `read-only`, or `add-only` into the spec folder, gives `run`. Everything else gives `start`.\n169: - **Evidence.** The repository already ships this split for a model-chosen command: `/doctor:helper` \"runs a read-only pick under that command's own router and prints a writing pick as one line for the operator\" (`routes:458`), and a default target is \"never on a mutating route\" (`routes:46-50`). A tracked-file edit is the top tier of the reversibility ladder, \"Proceed\" (`.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md:63`), and \"an install still waits for a yes\" (`:64`). Gate 3 fires on every write whatever the command does (`AGENTS.md:73`), so a second question before it breaks the single-prompt rule in `AGENTS.md` section 2. An omitted mode lands on confirm or a mode question (C27).\n170: - **How this settles the lineage split.** The strict line asks 34 times, mostly for writes the user's own sentence requested. The ladder line runs `/speckit:implement` with no question. The merged \"start\" is right for the middle band but wrong to lean on the command's setup alone (C30). The rule above keeps \"start\" only where an independent gate exists, and asks where none does.\n171: - **Judgment.** The middle band is a preference, see section 7. Would change it: an R8 behaviour run where a started create or speckit command skips its setup, or an unsafe-start rate above zero.\n172: - **Depends on.** R1.\n173: - **Proof.** The deriver emits a policy for all 44 tracked commands and every doctor route. A table-driven test asserts the four mechanical rows. Changing a doctor route from `read-only` to `mutates` flips its policy to `ask` in the generated file.\n174: \n175: ### R5. Invocation per runtime\n176: \n177: - **Merged text.** One command identity with a per-runtime adapter. Use a model-invocable command where the host has one. Elsewhere load the command document and follow it, or ask the user to type it. Derive the runtime list from the hook registrations.\n178: - **Verdict: AMEND. Priority: must.**\n179: - **Change.** Make \"read the command document in full and follow it\" the single instruction on every runtime. Treat Claude Code's Skill tool as an optional shortcut for the 33 names it lists. Never tell the model to type a slash command. Ask the user to type one only when the model cannot read the file. Document the per-runtime table from section 2.8 in `skill-advisor-hook.md` and add the DSH row.\n180: - **Evidence.** Section 2.8. The typed path on Codex, Pi and DSH is already this instruction (C35). The Skill tool misses 12 create commands (C33).\n181: - **Depends on.** R3.\n182: - **Proof.** A behaviour run per runtime: a chat prompt that should reach `/create:readme` ends with `create-readme` workflow YAML read before the first write. On Claude Code the same holds for a create command the Skill tool does not list.\n183: \n184: ### R6. The Gate 2 sentence\n185: \n186: - **Merged text.** Three layers: the brief carries command and policy, one sentence in Gate 2 binds the behaviour, the command file is the body.\n187: - **Verdict: AMEND. Priority: must. Not sufficient alone.**\n188: - **Change.** Add one bullet to Gate 2 in `AGENTS.md` after line 67. Proposed text, about 430 bytes:\n189: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 190]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/synthesis-review.md\",\"offset\":100,\"limit\":90}"},"time":{"start":1794532181504,"end":1794532182002}}}}
